{
  "headline": "President Mahama arrives in Moscow for third Russia–Africa Summit",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama of Ghana arrived in Moscow on Monday to lead a delegation to the third Russia–Africa Summit. The summit, hosted by Russian President Vladimir Putin, is scheduled for October 28–29, 2026, and is expected to include more than 30 African heads of state and government. According to presidential aide Yuri Ushakov, 48 African countries have confirmed their participation. The summit is expected to focus on peace diplomacy, security cooperation, and equitable economic partnerships. President Mahama is also scheduled to participate in an Economic and Humanitarian Forum and is expected to hold high-level bilateral talks with President Putin on the margins of the summit, a prospect confirmed by Foreign Affairs Minister Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa. Additionally, President Mahama is expected to grant an audience to various Russian businesses and state institutions, and two Russian universities—the Russian University of Transport and the Peoples’ Friendship University of Russia—are scheduled to separately confer honorary doctorate degrees on him during ceremonies in Moscow. Ghana's Ambassador to Russia, Dr Koma Steem Jehu-Appiah, led embassy staff to receive the President at the airport, where Russian government officials were also present."
    },
    {
      "text": "Speaking at the Financial Times Africa Summit on October 21, President Mahama stated he would use the Moscow visit to attempt to convince President Putin to agree to a ceasefire in the four-year-old Russia–Ukraine war. He framed this appeal within Ghana's non-aligned foreign policy, describing the nation as being among the 'friends to all and enemies to none.' The President noted that Accra maintains cordial relations with the United States, the European Union, China, and Russia, arguing that this balanced posture allows for engagement with Moscow on peace matters without taking sides. Separately, Moscow is hosting the eighth summit of the Gas Exporting Countries Forum on October 27 to discuss global energy market developments. The Russia–Africa Summit is expected to be co-chaired by President Putin and Burundi’s President Évariste Ndayishimiye, the current chair of the African Union."
    }
  ]
}

{"headline": "Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva wins Brazil presidential runoff election", "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "Incumbent President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva has secured a fourth term in Brazil following a second-round presidential runoff election. According to the Superior Electoral Court, Lula received 51.79% of the valid votes, defeating challenger Flávio Bolsonaro, who received 48.21%. This result marks the first time in Brazilian electoral history that a first-round verdict has been overturned, as Bolsonaro had led in the first round of voting earlier this month. The victory makes Lula the country's longest-serving leader. Following the announcement, thousands of supporters celebrated in cities such as São Paulo, where crowds gathered on Paulista Avenue on October 25, 2026. Flávio Bolsonaro, the son of former president Jair Bolsonaro, has refused to accept the result and stated he would seek to have it annulled. The former president, Jair Bolsonaro, is currently serving a 27-year jail term for his role in an attempted coup following the previous presidential election. During the election process, a Supreme Court judge extended polling time by two hours after both candidates requested the extension. Additionally, Lula's campaign accused opponents of sabotaging public transport to prevent voters from reaching the polls."
  },
  {
    "text": "The election result triggered significant volatility in Brazilian financial markets. Following the announcement, the Brazilian real dropped 12 percent against the United States dollar in spot trading. On Monday, Bovespa stock index futures tumbled nearly 15 percent, and the Bovespa index fell 10 percent from its previous close, triggering a 30-minute trading halt via a circuit-breaker mechanism. Rachel Ziemba, an adjunct senior fellow at the Center for a New American Security, told Al Jazeera that traders are covering bets that assumed a Bolsonaro victory and tighter fiscal policy, noting that market pressure is amplified by Bolsonaro's refusal to concede. This follows a period of market growth; on October 23, 2026, stocks closed almost 7 percent above their levels prior to the first round, while the real was nearly 5 percent stronger against the dollar."
  }
]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh and Japan seek expanded trade and investment ties during official visit",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "During a four-day official visit to Tokyo that began on October 26, 2026, Bangladesh Prime Minister Tarique Rahman and Japanese officials discussed strengthening bilateral trade and investment. The visit, made at the invitation of Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi, included a meeting between Tarique and Masumi Kakinoki, chairman of Marubeni Corporation and president of the Japan-Bangladesh Committee for Commercial and Economic Cooperation. Kakinoki stated that the effective implementation of the Economic Partnership Agreement could expand bilateral trade, increase Japanese investment, and create new business opportunities, according to BSS reports. Upon his arrival, Tarique was received by Japanese State Minister for Foreign Affairs Yasutaka Nakasone. Tarique is scheduled to hold a bilateral summit with Takaichi on October 27, 2026."
    },
    {
      "text": "Tarique sought Japanese investment to modernize the Karnaphuli Fertiliser Company Limited and to explore establishing a second fertilizer factory in Bangladesh. He also requested investment in advanced manufacturing, renewable energy, and information and communication technology, noting that the government is working to reform regulations and simplify approval procedures to create an investment-friendly environment. Officials expect the two countries to sign between five and eight agreements covering sectors such as agriculture, civil aviation, infrastructure, energy cooperation, and official security assistance for the Bangladesh Coast Guard. These agreements are expected to include memorandums of understanding regarding upazila-level development and business climate improvements."
    },
    {
      "text": "According to officials, the two nations are expected to agree on approximately $2 billion in loans and financial support, which includes $300 million in budgetary support. While Bangladesh is expected to seek development cooperation and investment, Japan is likely to focus on energy, maritime security, and regional connectivity. Additionally, the Japanese power generation company JERA is expected to assist in developing energy reserve infrastructure under the Moheshkhali Integrated Development Initiative."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "JoyNews investigation examines discrepancies in South Africa evacuation payment records",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A JoyNews Research investigation has identified what appear to be forged signatures and discrepancies in payment records related to a government-funded evacuation of hundreds of Ghanaians from South Africa. The investigation follows the government's decision to facilitate the return of these citizens and has raised questions regarding how public funds were spent during the operation. Specifically, the research examines whether the records submitted by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs accurately reflect the payments made and how documents containing questionable signatures became part of the data used to account for the expenditure."
    }
  ]
}

