}

{
  "headline": "Syrian Airlines to resume direct flights between Berlin and Damascus in November",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Syrian Airlines announced on October 26, 2026, that direct flights between Berlin and Damascus will resume next month. The first nonstop flight is scheduled to take off on Nov. 10, marking the airline’s first service connecting Germany with the Syrian Arab Republic in more than a decade. The airline had previously halted flights to Europe during the Syrian civil war, which began in 2011 and ended in December 2024 following the fall of President Bashar Assad and his regime during an opposition advance. According to the Syrian Arab News Agency, Syria’s General Authority for Civil Aviation and Air Transport stated the decision would make it easier for the approximately 1 million Syrians living in Germany to visit or return to their home country."
    },
    {
      "text": "The resumption of service follows recent activity by German carriers. In July, Leav Aviation began flying from Dusseldorf to Damascus, and in August, Sundair launched its first direct flight between Berlin and Damascus. During the civil war, major international airlines canceled flights to Syria, and travelers were often forced to travel overland to airports in Lebanon and Jordan to fly abroad."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "BNP Paribas Asset Management increases positions in long-dated Japanese government bonds",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "BNP Paribas Asset Management has been increasing its positions in long-dated Japanese government bonds (JGBs), citing a steep yield curve and indications of returning domestic demand in the market. James McAlevey, the firm's head of global aggregate and absolute return, stated during a briefing in Tokyo last week that the company has grown its long-end JGB position from a “very small size” earlier this year to a “moderate to medium size.” The firm is favoring 20- and 30-year bonds. McAlevey noted that the sector has recently “significantly outperformed other global long ends,” offering a source of diversification."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ga Mantse to chair 30th GJA Awards in Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "King Tackie Teiko Tsuru II, the Ga Mantse, is scheduled to chair the 30th anniversary edition of the Ghana Journalists Association (GJA) Awards. On October 23, 2026, GJA President Albert Kwabena Dwumfour led a national executive delegation to the Ga Mantse's palace in Kaneshie, Accra, to formally extend the invitation. The ceremony is scheduled to take place at the Accra International Conference Centre’s Grand Arena. While the Ga Mantse will chair the event, the Asantehene, Otumfuo Osei Tutu II, serves as the patron for the awards, marking the first time two prominent traditional rulers will share the spotlight at a single GJA event. The GJA selected the Ga Mantse for the role, citing his previous mediation of a 2021 election dispute that stalled the association's national polls for nearly a year. The 30th edition marks three decades since the GJA instituted the awards to recognize excellence in journalism."
    }
  ]
}
